{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>וַיֹּאמֶר אֶהְיֶה etc.</b> Meaning: He made known to him the attribute by which He speaks with him, and this name is related to the attribute of mercy, which is the aspect of bringing forth from slavery to freedom. Go out and learn from what they said in Sefer HaTikkunim (Tikkun 30) — the sod of the fifty times the Exodus from Egypt is mentioned in the Torah — and this is also the reason the Egyptians suffered fifty plagues, as they of blessed memory said (Shemot Rabbah 5). And this name, El Elyon already alluded to it in the pleasantness of His words when He said to him: ״כִּי אֶהְיֶה עִמָּךָ״. And perhaps it is for this reason that He leaned and said <b>וְזֶה לְּךָ הָאוֹת כִּי אָנֹכִי שְׁלַחְתִּיךָ</b> — meaning, the aspect of ״אָנֹכִי,״ and this is that HaShem at Mount Sinai opened His words with ״אָנֹכִי etc. אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ״ etc., except that Moses did not understand the depth of the words, for HaShem knew that Moses would come to ask about the matter, and He gave the utterance of the herald of a great host — the aspect that speaks and its sod." "Or perhaps Moses did understand and comprehend, but he asks whether he should mention this name to Israel. And His saying <b>אֲשֶׁר אֶהְיֶה</b> is the reason for the name that is so called on account of His being with Israel in their distress, as they of blessed memory said (Chagigah 12a): the reason for the name Shaddai — that He said to His world ״enough״ (dai); the reason for Tzevaot — that He is a sign in His host (tzeva) (Chagigah 16a); the reason for the Name of Havayah — on account of He was, He is, and He will be (Zohar III 287a). So too אֶהְיֶה — on account of He will be, and this is His saying <b>אֲשֶׁר אֶהְיֶה</b>, and here He made known to Moses the sod of the great Name. And our Sages of blessed memory (Berakhot 9a) interpreted: אֶהְיֶה — I will be with them in this distress; אֲשֶׁר אֶהְיֶה — I will be with them in another distress. Also there is in this a sod of three crowns, and the discerning one will understand." "<b>וַיֹּאמֶר כֹּה תֹאמַר etc.</b> Meaning: Although He revealed to Moses His servant the sod of the Name as He said ״אֲשֶׁר אֶהְיֶה,״ He went back to say that you should not say this in speech but only the mention of the Name — and this is also His saying <b>אֶהְיֶה שְׁלָחַנִי אֲלֵיכֶם</b> — but what I added to reveal to you, ״אֲשֶׁר אֶהְיֶה,״ is on condition that you not say it." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berakhot 9a) that Moses turned back to his Master with the argument ״it is enough for distress in its hour.״ I peered and saw that they spoke a word of wisdom and justice from the force of the verse itself, for if the end of the speech were like the beginning of the speech and there was no retreat from the first utterance — but rather He who completes His words to His faithful prophet in His house — there would have been no reason for Him to say ״וַיֹּאמֶר״ in the middle of the first utterance. Therefore they said that HaShem blessed be He, in saying ״אֶהְיֶה אֲשֶׁר אֶהְיֶה,״ finished the utterance of the matter, and for a certain reason went back to say another utterance. And the discerning one will understand the difference between the first utterance and the second utterance and the One who speaks to him, and will know the motivating cause and the restrainer." "And if you say: how was it that at first HaShem did not attend to the matter, so that Moses needed to say before Him etc.? — behold, in what we examine closely in His blessed words, you will know that He who knows hidden things of wisdom attended to a certain matter: that it was necessary to say at the beginning of His words <b>כֹּה תֹאמַר לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל</b>, since this response comes in answer to Moses's argument ״וְאָמְרוּ לִי מַה שְּׁמוֹ״, He should have had to answer him ״כֹּה תֹאמַר.״ But since He knew that this matter would not stand as is because of what Moses would argue afterward — ״it is enough for distress״ — therefore, so that His words would not come to naught, He did not say ״כֹּה תֹאמַר,״ and instead spoke His words evenly, such that there is in their meaning that everything is a response to ״what shall I say to them.״ And when Moses came and argued, He said that you shall say only ״אֶהְיֶה,״ and what He said ״אֲשֶׁר אֶהְיֶה״ is a revelation to Moses, and also a revelation to future generations, so that their hearts may be sustained when they see that He who dwells in the bush will be with Israel in every distress. And had He spelled out His words from the start and said ״כֹּה תֹאמַר etc. אֶהְיֶה שְׁלָחַנִי אֲלֵיכֶם,״ this knowledge would have been lacking from Israel, in such a manner that the beginning of the thought would have been the end of the utterance. But Moses lacked a little from Elohim; or perhaps he did know and perceive, but he did not rely on his own reasoning and wanted HaShem to reveal His mind to him explicitly."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:23.246123512Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}